    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! Traditionalist theologians often argue that marriage has a divinely ordained purpose, referring to it as a sacrament or covenant between husband and wife, which is ideally intended for procreation and nurturing within a stable family unit. They may criticize modern secular notions that regard marriage as a more flexible social contract, expanding it to same-sex couples, or shifting the grounds of consent over community responsibility. These theologians assert that by expanding marriage's definition, society undermines its foundation and that it defies natural law by neglecting the roles and responsibilities bestowed upon genders. They are also concerned that redefining marriage may detract from its spiritual significance and moral guidance.",
