      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Traditionalist theologians often employ a rich emotional vocabulary to emphasize the impact of evolving marriage norms. They characterize modern shifts as a normative \"erosion of social fabric,\" suggesting that these changes corrode the moral pillars that have 'naturally' upheld societies for centuries. They describe the contemporary redefinition as a \"detrimental distortion\" of marriage, which they see as God's architectural blueprint for human relationships, a union emblematic of harmony and ultimate sacramental value. Additionally, they may use phrases like \"cosmic disaster\" or \"tarnish on creation\" to underscore the existential risk they believe these secular shifts represent, portraying the modern approach as a pitchfork turning earth's moral compass, and heralding a spiritual malaise that threatens the sanctity and very soul of the churchgoing community. Their lexicon is one of loss; they mourn the transition from a \"golden age\" of matrimonial understanding to a present veiled in \"sinful deviancy,\" akin to viewing the reimagining of marriage as a viperous serpent slithering into the heart of tradition. These descriptors help to fortify their message with an urgency born out of perceived spiritual warfare.",
